// FAIL CANARY. Two mutable element loans under a STATED `left_index !=
// right_index`. The language guide's Borrow Facts section
// (wiki/language_guide/chapter_2_ownership_borrowing_moves.md) names exactly
// this shape -- "Bounds and `i != j` can establish that the elements differ"
// -- so the capability is intended, but no premise form carries it today.
//
// This is `fail/borrows/borrow_unknown_index_pair_mut` with one variable
// moved: that fixture states no relation between the two indexes, this one
// states disequality. Both reject identically, which is the point -- the
// stated premise buys nothing yet.
//
// The indexes are declared `[0..=1]` against the length-2 array so the bounds
// obligations discharge and the borrow refusal is the ONLY diagnostic left.
// Without those ranges the fixture also reports `cannot prove index ... is
// within length 2` twice, and a reader could not tell which refusal it pins.
// Do not drop them to "simplify" the fixture.
//
// Why it cannot be admitted by ordinary engineering: the premise vocabulary
// is ordering-only. `BorrowCompatibilityPremiseRelation`
// (checked-trees/src/checked_trees/borrow.rs) is the closed triple
// `LessOrEqual`/`StrictlyBefore`/`Equal`, and its own doc scopes it to
// integer-bound ordering. `decompose_premise_expression`
// (checks/borrows/overlap/premises.rs) has no `NotEqual` arm, and
// `premise_orientation_proves` is a total 3x3 match in constant-offset shifts
// (`left_shift <= right_shift`) -- disequality orders nothing, so it has no
// cell there. The only other derivation, `Structural`, retains no premises.
// Admitting this needs a fourth variant on a PERSISTED certificate relation
// that Terminal replay consumes positionally, which is an owner decision
// about proof vocabulary rather than a change within it.
//
// When that decision lands, this canary moves to `pass/` unchanged.
data Entry {
    value: i32;
}

data Main {
    entries: [Entry; 2];
}

machine Main::main(&mut self, left_index: u64 [0..=1], right_index: u64 [0..=1])
requires
    left_index != right_index
{
    let view: &mut [Entry] = self.entries.as_mut_slice();
    let left: &mut Entry = &mut view[left_index];
    let right: &mut Entry = &mut view[right_index];
    left = Entry { value: 1 };
    right = Entry { value: 2 };
}
